WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:01.668
[music playing]

00:00:01.735 --> 00:00:02.969
So the terrain here
is definitely getting

00:00:03.036 --> 00:00:04.471
steeper and more committing.

00:00:04.537 --> 00:00:06.606
But you know, so
much of survival

00:00:06.673 --> 00:00:09.409
is about just trying
to be resourceful.

00:00:09.476 --> 00:00:16.883
I just wonder if maybe we
maybe use that old trunk.

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:21.287
Use that, get that down, and
then we can down climb that.

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:22.655
Let's have a look at it, anyway.

00:00:22.722 --> 00:00:23.790
Yeah.

00:00:23.857 --> 00:00:25.325
[laughs] Is there some
way we can use our ax

00:00:25.392 --> 00:00:26.659
to make the ladder out of that?

00:00:26.726 --> 00:00:27.527
Yeah, brilliant idea.

00:00:27.594 --> 00:00:28.161
Brilliant.

00:00:28.228 --> 00:00:29.195
[laughs]

00:00:29.262 --> 00:00:30.530
BEAR GRYLLS: Totally.

00:00:30.597 --> 00:00:32.732
Maybe if we, like, cut
little steps into it--

00:00:32.799 --> 00:00:34.868
it doesn't have to be
big, you know, just enough

00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:37.170
to use as hands and feet.

00:00:37.237 --> 00:00:39.439
Drag it, drop it down.

00:00:39.506 --> 00:00:41.775
Maybe tie this rope to the end
of it so it stays in place.

00:00:41.841 --> 00:00:43.042
Sure.

00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:45.779
We passed this old
log on the way down.

00:00:45.845 --> 00:00:46.946
Have you got that ax on you?

00:00:47.013 --> 00:00:47.947
Yeah.

00:00:48.014 --> 00:00:49.349
Let's bring the ax up here.

00:00:49.416 --> 00:00:50.984
And of course, because the
air is so dry out here,

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:53.086
nothing really rots.

00:00:53.153 --> 00:00:56.189
Rock hard, though, this wood.

00:00:56.256 --> 00:00:58.425
But whatever it was
used for one time,

00:00:58.491 --> 00:01:00.293
we're going to use it
for our purpose, which

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:01.828
is getting down this crack.

00:01:01.895 --> 00:01:04.130
So if you could give us the
ax, what we're trying to do

00:01:04.197 --> 00:01:05.331
is trying to get--

00:01:05.398 --> 00:01:07.000
We can cut some
little steps into it,

00:01:07.066 --> 00:01:10.503
and drop it down this crack,
and use those, and down climb.

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:13.773
[chopping]

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:16.242
[music playing]

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:21.414
Let's get you doing
a bit of that.

00:01:21.481 --> 00:01:24.150
I'm going to get this rope
ready, and then we're into it.

00:01:24.217 --> 00:01:25.685
DAVE BAUTISTA: Bear's
plan is to somehow

00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:28.521
climb down this log
ladder that we've built

00:01:28.588 --> 00:01:30.457
from our two hands and an ax.

00:01:30.523 --> 00:01:32.525
[laughs]

00:01:32.592 --> 00:01:33.359
BEAR GRYLLS: OK.

00:01:33.426 --> 00:01:35.795
So that's a bunch of steps cut.

00:01:35.862 --> 00:01:39.165
And now, what we've got
to do is position it.

00:01:39.232 --> 00:01:40.667
So if you take the heavy end--

00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:42.335
All right.

00:01:42.402 --> 00:01:43.703
I'll this end.
You read?

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:44.704
Heading that way?

00:01:44.771 --> 00:01:45.772
We're going down to the right.
Yeah.

00:01:45.839 --> 00:01:46.606
DAVE BAUTISTA: Yep.

00:01:46.673 --> 00:01:47.707
Yeah.

00:01:47.774 --> 00:01:48.641
BEAR GRYLLS: Oh, my god.

00:01:48.708 --> 00:01:50.610
[laughs] Bautista,
you crack me up.

00:01:50.677 --> 00:01:52.345
Here we go, right?

00:01:52.412 --> 00:01:55.181
It's literally like
he's moving a matchstick.

00:01:55.248 --> 00:01:57.584
This is so heavy.

00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:00.386
Well, the great thing
about having Dave Bautista

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:01.654
on this journey is
that when you're

00:02:01.721 --> 00:02:04.958
moving heavy tree trunks
around, it suddenly all

00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:06.993
becomes a little bit easier.

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:08.461
OK, watch your step down there.

00:02:08.528 --> 00:02:10.630
We'll let gravity do the
work now, if you put it down.

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:15.134
And then we'll come up this end.

00:02:17.937 --> 00:02:19.239
DAVE BAUTISTA: I
feel OK climbing

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:22.141
down this log right now.

00:02:22.208 --> 00:02:24.477
But as Mike Tyson
said, everybody

00:02:24.544 --> 00:02:26.846
always has a plan until they
get punched in the face.

00:02:26.913 --> 00:02:27.714
So I'll see--

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.215
[laughs]

00:02:29.282 --> 00:02:30.650
--how it starts looking when
we're actually doing this.

00:02:30.717 --> 00:02:33.453
But as of right now,
I'm extremely confident.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:35.154
[laughs]

00:02:35.221 --> 00:02:37.257
So when I go down, am
I going down, like,

00:02:37.323 --> 00:02:38.358
keeping my weight on the rope?

00:02:38.424 --> 00:02:40.193
Or should I try
to balance myself?

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:41.394
A bit of both.

00:02:41.461 --> 00:02:42.562
- A bit of both.
- A bit of both, yeah.

00:02:42.629 --> 00:02:44.030
As you get steeper,
it's going to be

00:02:44.097 --> 00:02:45.031
more natural to use
your hands and stuff

00:02:45.098 --> 00:02:46.199
and your feet as well.
So--

00:02:46.266 --> 00:02:47.500
DAVE BAUTISTA: OK.

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:48.167
OK.

00:02:48.234 --> 00:02:51.170
[suspenseful music]

00:02:55.275 --> 00:02:58.177
OK, tell me when
you're at the top.

00:02:58.244 --> 00:02:59.045
Not yet.

00:02:59.112 --> 00:02:59.846
Almost.

00:03:03.149 --> 00:03:04.584
OK, I got my foot on it.

00:03:04.651 --> 00:03:06.653
OK, now try and use the
log because I'm going to--

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:08.755
I'm struggling to hold
you on that one side.

00:03:08.821 --> 00:03:09.589
OK.

00:03:09.656 --> 00:03:10.523
[grunts]

00:03:14.561 --> 00:03:15.762
BEAR GRYLLS: Keep talking to me.

00:03:15.828 --> 00:03:16.596
Are you on there?

00:03:16.663 --> 00:03:17.297
I can't see you now.

00:03:17.363 --> 00:03:18.431
Yeah, I'm on the log.

00:03:21.501 --> 00:03:24.704
And these notches are much
farther apart than I thought.

00:03:24.771 --> 00:03:25.638
[laughs]

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:33.880
OK, I'm down.

00:03:33.947 --> 00:03:35.248
I made it.

00:03:35.315 --> 00:03:36.549
OK, good job.

00:03:36.616 --> 00:03:40.386
OK, I'm going to follow
you down this line.

00:03:40.453 --> 00:03:41.821
That were a pretty
good one there.

00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:42.655
DAVE BAUTISTA: Yeah.

00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:44.257
[laughs]

00:03:45.692 --> 00:03:46.392
Yeah, look at that.

00:03:46.459 --> 00:03:47.860
DAVE BAUTISTA: [laughs]

00:03:47.927 --> 00:03:49.162
Good job.

00:03:49.228 --> 00:03:50.563
Well done.

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:52.031
When I got a little high,
it was a little scary.

00:03:52.098 --> 00:03:53.366
And there was a
couple times where

00:03:53.433 --> 00:03:54.367
I just wanted to wrap
myself around the log

00:03:54.434 --> 00:03:55.435
and hopefully slide down.

